{"id":"0bb4aeed-debf-4b9f-9466-a70e3f6a637c","object":"chat.completion","created":1784885949,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    core = case['core_distance']\\n    raw_dist_list = case['distances']\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n not in core:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Node {n} missing core_distance\\\")\\n    raw = {}\\n    for n1, n2, d in raw_dist_list:\\n        if n1 not in nodes or n2 not in nodes:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Unknown node in distances\\\")\\n        a, b = (n1, n2) if n1 <= n2 else (n2, n1)\\n        raw[(a,b)] = d\\n    edges = []\\n    n = len(nodes)\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes)\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        u = sorted_nodes[i]\\n        for j in range(i+1, n):\\n            v = sorted_nodes[j]\\n            d = raw.get((u,v))\\n            if d is None:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Missing distance between {u} and {v}\\\")\\n            mr = max(core[u], core[v], d)\\n            edges.append((u, v, mr))\\n    edges.sort(key=lambda x: (x[2], x[0], x[1]))\\n    parent = {}\\n    rank = {}\\n    def find(x):\\n        while parent[x] != x:\\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\\n            x = parent[x]\\n        return x\\n    def union(x, y):\\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\\n        if rx == ry:\\n            return False\\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\\n            parent[rx] = ry\\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n        else:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n            rank[rx] += 1\\n        return True\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        parent[node] = node\\n        rank[node] = 0\\n    mst_edges = []\\n    total_weight = 0.0\\n    for u, v, w in edges:\\n        if union(u, v):\\n            mst_edges.append([u, v, w])\\n            total_weight += w\\n            if len(mst_edges) == n - 1:\\n                break\\n    if len(mst_edges) != n - 1:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Graph is disconnected; cannot form MST\\\")\\n    return {\\n        \\\"mst_edges\\\": mst_edges,\\n        \\\"total_weight\\\": total_weight\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that given a payload with nodes, core_distance dict, and distances list (each [node1, node2, distance]), we compute mutual reachability distances: for each pair (u,v) we have core distances cu, cv, raw distance d; mutual_reachability = max(cu, cv, d). Then we need to build a minimum spanning tree (MST) using Kruskal's algorithm on the complete graph (all pairs of nodes except self?). The nodes list gives the set of nodes; distances list gives raw distances for some pairs? Actually the distances list includes all pairs? The examples show 10 entries for 5 nodes, which is complete graph. So we can assume distances contain all pairs, but we must handle possible missing? Probably it's complete. But to be safe, we should compute mutual reachability for all pairs of nodes. We have the raw distance from the distances list. However, distances list may not include all pairs symmetrically? It appears each pair appears once (undirected). So we can build a dictionary from (min_node, max_node) to distance. Then iterate over all unordered pairs of nodes.\n\nImplementation steps in solve(case):\n1. Validate inputs: case is dict with keys 'nodes' (list of strings), 'core_distance' (dict mapping node->float), 'distances' (list of [node1, node2, distance]).\n2. Build a dict of raw distances: for each entry, ensure both nodes in nodes set, canonicalize (sort pair), store distance.\n3. For each unordered pair (i, j) where i != j, compute mutual_reachability = max(core_distance[node_i], core_distance[node_j], raw_distance[i,j]).\n4. Create list of edges: (node_i, node_j, weight). Sort edges by weight, then by node_i, then by node_j (lexicographically). The tie-breaking: \"Sort by reachability then endpoint IDs.\" Since we are not given an explicit order for endpoint IDs beyond lexicographic? The problem says \"Canonicalize undirected endpoints.\" Probably means store (min, max) lex order. Then for tie-breaking, sort by weight, then by first node, then by second node.\n5. Kruskal with Union-Find (disjoint set). Union-Find with path compression and union by rank/size.\n6. Iterate through sorted edges; for each, if find(u) != find(v): union, add edge to mst_edges, add weight to total_weight.\n7. Stop when mst_edges has len(nodes)-1 edges. If we exhaust all edges without reaching n-1, raise ValueError (disconnected graph - but since it's complete graph, it's always connected? Possibly raw distances could be missing making some pairs not present? But we can assume complete. However the spec says \"Reject disconnected inputs\". We can check after loop: if len(mst_edges) != len(nodes)-1: raise ValueError).\n8. Return {\"mst_edges\": mst_edges (list of [u,v,weight]), \"total_weight\": total_weight}.\n9. Ensure edges in mst_edges are in the order they were added (Kruskal order), which is deterministic due to sorting.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Use Python's json module to serialize? Actually solve returns dict, which should be JSON-serializable.\n- No imports beyond standard library (allowed: json, but not needed). Use built-in types.\n\nWe must write the implementation as a string inside a JSON object with key \"implementation\". The string must be valid Python source code. Ensure to escape characters in JSON string (backslashes, quotes, newlines). I'll produce Python code with proper indentation.\n\nTesting with given payloads? Not required, but we can mentally verify.\n\nEdge Cases: core_distance may have entries for all nodes? Yes. nodes list defines nodes. If any node missing core_distance, raise error. Also if distances list doesn't contain a pair, we could treat as missing? But spec says \"distances\" list contains all pairs. We'll assume complete.\n\nImplementation function:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    core = case['core_distance']\n    raw_dist_list = case['distances']\n    # validate nodes in core\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n not in core:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Node {n} missing core_distance\")\n    # build raw distance dict\n    raw = {}\n    for n1, n2, d in raw_dist_list:\n        if n1 not in nodes or n2 not in nodes:\n            raise ValueError(\"Unknown node in distances\")\n        a, b = (n1, n2) if n1 <= n2 else (n2, n1)\n        raw[(a,b)] = d\n    # compute mutual reachability edges\n    edges = []\n    n = len(nodes)\n    # sort nodes for deterministic pair iteration\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes)\n    for i in range(n):\n        u = sorted_nodes[i]\n        for j in range(i+1, n):\n            v = sorted_nodes[j]\n            d = raw.get((u,v))\n            # if missing, we could raise error? For complete graph assume present\n            if d is None:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Missing distance between {u} and {v}\")\n            mr = max(core[u], core[v], d)\n            edges.append((u, v, mr))\n    # sort edges: (weight, u, v)\n    edges.sort(key=lambda x: (x[2], x[0], x[1]))\n    # Union-Find\n    parent = {}\n    rank = {}\n    def find(x):\n        while parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\n            x = parent[x]\n        return x\n    def union(x, y):\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\n        if rx == ry:\n            return False\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\n            parent[rx] = ry\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\n            parent[ry] = rx\n        else:\n            parent[ry] = rx\n            rank[rx] += 1\n        return True\n    # initialize\n    for node in nodes:\n        parent[node] = node\n        rank[node] = 0\n    mst_edges = []\n    total_weight = 0.0\n    for u, v, w in edges:\n        if union(u, v):\n            mst_edges.append([u, v, w])\n            total_weight += w\n            if len(mst_edges) == n - 1:\n                break\n    if len(mst_edges) != n - 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"Graph is disconnected; cannot form MST\")\n    return {\n        \"mst_edges\": mst_edges,\n        \"total_weight\": total_weight\n    }\n\nNote: Use float for weights.\n\nWe need to ensure the output matches schema exactly: mst_edges is list of arrays each [string, string, number] (max 3 min 3). total_weight number.\n\nNow produce the response JSON with key \"implementation\" and value as escaped Python string. We'll generate the JSON manually.\n\nWe'll write the Python code as a multi-line string, then escape for JSON. Let me construct the string.\n\nI'll keep the code compact but readable. Use single quotes for strings to minimize escaping in JSON? But JSON requires backslash escaping for double quotes inside string. Use double quotes for Python strings? It's fine; we can use triple single quotes for multiline string. But within the JSON string, we need to escape backslashes and double quotes. Let's use triple double quotes for Python code? Actually it's simpler to write the Python code using single quotes for all strings and avoid unnecessary escaping. But the code uses double quotes for dict keys (case['nodes'] etc). That's fine; we just need to escape double quotes in JSON by backslash. Also newlines as \\n. I'll produce the code as a single-line string with explicit \\n escapes. That might be easier. But to preserve readability, I can use actual newlines in the JSON string, which is allowed. JSON can contain newline characters in string values (escaped as \\n or literal newline? According to JSON spec, newlines in strings must be escaped as \\n. Literal newline is not allowed. So we must replace newlines with \\n. So either write one-liner or use \\n escapes.\n\nI'll write the code as a single line but with semicolons? Better to use \\n for readability. I'll construct a string with \\n.\n\nAlternatively, I can construct the JSON using Python's json.dumps, but since this is the output, I need to produce it manually. I'll write the Python code in a block, then escape newlines and quotes manually.\n\nLet me produce the implementation code string:\n\n\"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    core = case['core_distance']\\n    raw_dist_list = case['distances']\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n not in core:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Node {n} missing core_distance\\\")\\n    raw = {}\\n    for n1, n2, d in raw_dist_list:\\n        if n1 not in nodes or n2 not in nodes:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Unknown node in distances\\\")\\n        a, b = (n1, n2) if n1 <= n2 else (n2, n1)\\n        raw[(a,b)] = d\\n    edges = []\\n    n = len(nodes)\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes)\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        u = sorted_nodes[i]\\n        for j in range(i+1, n):\\n            v = sorted_nodes[j]\\n            d = raw.get((u,v))\\n            if d is None:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Missing distance between {u} and {v}\\\")\\n            mr = max(core[u], core[v], d)\\n            edges.append((u, v, mr))\\n    edges.sort(key=lambda x: (x[2], x[0], x[1]))\\n    parent = {}\\n    rank = {}\\n    def find(x):\\n        while parent[x] != x:\\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\\n            x = parent[x]\\n        return x\\n    def union(x, y):\\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\\n        if rx == ry:\\n            return False\\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\\n            parent[rx] = ry\\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n        else:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n            rank[rx] += 1\\n        return True\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        parent[node] = node\\n        rank[node] = 0\\n    mst_edges = []\\n    total_weight = 0.0\\n    for u, v, w in edges:\\n        if union(u, v):\\n            mst_edges.append([u, v, w])\\n            total_weight += w\\n            if len(mst_edges) == n - 1:\\n                break\\n    if len(mst_edges) != n - 1:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Graph is disconnected; cannot form MST\\\")\\n    return {\\n        \\\"mst_edges\\\": mst_edges,\\n        \\\"total_weight\\\": total_weight\\n    }\"\n\nNow escape double quotes inside: each double quote needs backslash. Also need to escape backslashes? Already none. Also need to escape any other special chars like tabs? None. So replace all \" with \\\". The code has double quotes in dict keys (case['nodes'] etc) - those are single quotes, safe. In raise ValueError(f\\\"Node ...\\\") - f-string uses double quotes, need escape. In \"Missing distance between...\" etc. Also in return dict keys \"mst_edges\", \"total_weight\". Also in function call union(u,v) etc. So escape those.\n\nLet me produce the final JSON. I'll write it as:\n\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    core = case['core_distance']\\n    raw_dist_list = case['distances']\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n not in core:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Node {n} missing core_distance\\\")\\n    raw = {}\\n    for n1, n2, d in raw_dist_list:\\n        if n1 not in nodes or n2 not in nodes:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Unknown node in distances\\\")\\n        a, b = (n1, n2) if n1 <= n2 else (n2, n1)\\n        raw[(a,b)] = d\\n    edges = []\\n    n = len(nodes)\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes)\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        u = sorted_nodes[i]\\n        for j in range(i+1, n):\\n            v = sorted_nodes[j]\\n            d = raw.get((u,v))\\n            if d is None:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Missing distance between {u} and {v}\\\")\\n            mr = max(core[u], core[v], d)\\n            edges.append((u, v, mr))\\n    edges.sort(key=lambda x: (x[2], x[0], x[1]))\\n    parent = {}\\n    rank = {}\\n    def find(x):\\n        while parent[x] != x:\\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\\n            x = parent[x]\\n        return x\\n    def union(x, y):\\n        rx, ry = find